WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.910
For every forgettable blockbuster that has
come and gone, there's been a steady stream

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.080
of stellar under-the-radar action flicks.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:11.260
What they lack in terms of scale and big-budget
effects, they make up for with their share

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.280
of bone-shattering brawls, white knuckle chases,
and bloody bombast.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.620
A few years back, writer/director Ted Geoghegan
made a name for himself among the genre set

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.630
with the atmospheric haunted house film We
Are Still Here.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.660
Though the film's no-budget production limited
what Geoghegan could ultimately accomplish,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.920
there was enough style on display to get excited
about what the filmmaker would do next.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:38.140
Surprisingly, instead of horror, Geoghegan
jumped to gory action in Mohawk.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.980
Mohawk unfolds in the last days of the War
of 1812, and finds a young Mohawk woman and

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.520
her lovers squaring off against a violent
band of American soldiers.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.120
Mohawk might flag a bit in dialogue-heavy
scenes, but when Geoghegan leaves words out

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.180
of the mix, this underseen actioner has as
much energy and vision as any blockbuster.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.720
If you've got an Amazon Prime subscription,
then you're probably aware of the streaming

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.720
service's new action series Hanna.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.320
What you might not be aware of is that the
show is based on a 2011 film of the same name.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.120
That film ended up doing moderately well at
the global box office, but it feels like it's

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.920
been all but forgotten by audiences in the
years since.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.560
Like the series, the film version of Hanna
follows a teenage girl who has a penchant

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.360
for putting down bad guys with a graceful
brutality.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.080
All the while, she's hunted by a secretive
intelligence operative a mirthfully menacing

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:28.980
Cate Blanchett.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:34.920
Hanna is a taut, boldly stylized pseudo-detective
action fantasy that delivers a weighty payload

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.580
in terms of both pulse-pounding set pieces
and character driven drama.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.520
It's high time this action-packed gem got
rediscovered.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.740
"I just missed your heart."

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.700
For many American moviegoers, exposure to
the work of Japanese extreme auteur Takashi

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.080
Miike begins and ends with his dark 1999 masterwork
Audition.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.960
Understandable as it may be, you're doing
yourself a disservice by ignoring his other

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:01.460
films.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.020
Among Miike's sprawling output, you'll find
the work of a visionary filmmaker with a flair

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.880
for genre, a penchant for gore, and plenty
of eye-popping action.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.600
You'll get plenty of all of that in Miike's
2010 samurai stunner 13 Assassins.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.319
Set in the waning days of Feudal Japan, the
film's setup promises a harrowing showdown

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:24.690
between the title assassins and the shogun's
ruthless brother, whose upcoming promotion

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.880
threatens to bring chaos to the otherwise
peaceful Japanese landscape.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:33.720
Miike takes his time getting to that showdown,
meticulously building toward the fateful finale

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.040
with a screenplay that allows audiences to
get good and comfy with the anti-heroes and

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.220
villains of the piece.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:45.540
When Miike finally blows the lid off 13 Assassins,
he does so with a bloody, brutal savagery

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.700
all but unseen in American cinema.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:53.460
Wheelman follows a freshly paroled getaway
driver played by Frank Grillo who finds himself

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.960
double-crossed and on the run.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:59.820
Fittingly for a movie following a character
played by long-time character actor Grillo,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.100
the film is very much the sort of pulpy crime
flick that plays to his strengths.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.640
Writer/director Jeremy Rush gives his star
ample room to talk tough, act tough, and drive

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.140
hard.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.500
Rush amplifies the film's inherent machismo
by keeping his camera confined almost entirely

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.740
within a single car.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.620
It's a bold, stylish move that pays off in
spades, giving a fresh perspective to the

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.140
sort of car-crashing, fist fighting, and machine
gunning we've seen a million times over.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.460
"What's your name, man?"

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.780
"I don't wanna talk."

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:32.780
"What?"

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:34.660
"Chitchat.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.460
I don't chitchat unless it's about the job."

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.040
One day, the world at large will catch up
to the genius of writer/director Joe Cornish.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.879
When it does, they'll likely look back on
2011's Attack The Block and wonder how it

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.660
ever became a box office bomb.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:53.120
Cornish's film delivers the action-packed
stoner comedy/alien invasion/horror flick

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.660
the world never knew it needed.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.960
Sadly, the world still seems oblivious to
this sharply written, exquisitely executed

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.360
genre mashup, despite the jaw-dropping action
scenes and killer creature design.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.040
Not to mention what should have been star-making
turns from future Doctor Who star Jodie Whitaker,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.520
and later Star Wars star John Boyega.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:15.160
Do yourself a favor and check out this hidden
gem.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:20.280
Though the bloodshed has finally stopped,
the simmering tensions that drove Irish Catholics

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.530
and Protestants headlong into the carnage
of "The Troubles" remains a part of life in

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:25.240
Northern Ireland.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:32.460
In Belfast circa 1971, the setting of '71,
the divisive fires that drove the madness

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:36.020
still burned with the raging heat of bombed
out cars and gunpowder.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:42.120
'71 opens with a kindly new recruit joining
a British military regiment in the war-torn

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.260
streets of Belfast.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:48.600
The film pivots sharply when an angry mob
ends up separating the man from his retreating

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:54.000
regiment, leaving him lost, alone, and injured
in a situation where he's perceived as enemy

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.300
to one and all.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.199
From there, the film plunges viewers into
a breathless, nightlong race for survival

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.220
through an unforgiving landscape where vengeance
is righteous no matter the cost.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.919
'71 is that rarest of action films that dares
to examine both the human cost of the bloody

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.380
conflict and the cycle of hate that fueled
it.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.700
"You should be ashamed of yourselves!

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:16.660
These boys are young enough to be your sons!"

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.080
In case you missed out on Haywire in theaters,
the film follows a tough-as-nails black ops

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.610
agent called Mallory who finds herself hunted
by international mercenaries after being double-crossed

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.840
on a clandestine mission.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:34.520
With plenty of grit, brawn, and brains, Mallory
quickly turns the tides on her betrayers,

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.440
setting up a hunted-becomes-the-hunter revenge
tale for the ages.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:43.270
Though Haywire marked director Steven Soderbergh's
first full-blown action flick, you'd never

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:47.720
be able to tell by how effortlessly he executes
the film's intricate set pieces.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.780
Each escalating act comes with a style and
whimsy most action movies never even aspire

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.280
to.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.199
That Soderbergh cast legit MMA star Gina Carano
in the lead role only serves to legitimize

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.620
each vicious escalation even as the film unapologetically
begins to earn its title.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.380
It all makes Haywire an overlooked diamond
among Soderbergh's impeccable filmography.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:12.350
If you'd have told us a few years back that
we'd be listing a certified fresh, Tom Cruise-fronted

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:17.610
sci-fi epic among our "underrated action movies"
picks, we probably would've told you you were

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.620
completely insane.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.919
Yet here we are, half a decade after its release,
still wondering how so many people slept on

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.980
this intelligent, action-packed banger.

00:06:27.389 --> 00:06:31.480
Edge of Tomorrow, which follows a soldier
continuously killed during a deadly alien

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:35.260
assault only to be dropped right back into
the action, is worth remembering.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:40.900
This Groundhog Day-esque action film is a
classic, one that matches epic human drama

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.740
against an equally epic scope.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:48.180
It's also a film with an insanely cool sci-fi
concept with mind boggling special effects

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.420
and set pieces that make repeat viewings endlessly
rewarding.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.500
If you haven't seen it, go watch it.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.360
If you have seen it, see it again.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:00.640
“I would go with the sense of manifest destiny,
the ranks, the rags to riches rapid rise through

00:07:00.659 --> 00:07:02.800
the ranks a born to deliver us...

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.020
The people… well they love that sort of
thing."

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:10.380
Few genres are as well primed to build big
time action into a narrative as science fiction.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.419
While Hollywood has given us its share of
supersized sci-fi bombast over the years,

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:19.689
audiences have proven themselves equally open
to experiencing more concept-driven science

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:20.420
fiction fare.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.180
While Leigh Whannell's clever, camp-tinged
techno thriller Upgrade sits firmly in the

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.120
latter category, this unpredictable tale wholly
embraces its big-budget ambition.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:35.300
It's the sort of savvy, small-scale science
fiction that deserved a much broader audience.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.020
If you're among those who missed Upgrade in
theaters, it follows a technophobic everyman

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.780
who blazes a path of bloody vengeance after
a mugging leaves him paralyzed and his wife

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:45.280
dead.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:50.240
He's aided in that endeavor by STEM, a computer
chips that allows him to walk, as well as

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.520
dole out plenty of violence on his attackers.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:58.129
From start to finish, Whannell spins a wholly
original, action-packed sci-fi tale from clear

00:07:58.129 --> 00:08:03.409
influences, all while having way more fun
with its haughty-yet-silly concept than most

00:08:03.409 --> 00:08:04.880
action movies would dare.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:09.020
"You are now in full control, Grey."

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:15.730
Let's take a moment to talk about Jeremy Saulnier's
harrowing survival drama Green Room, a film

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.820
that couldn't find its audience even with
a glowing review from Quentin Tarantino.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:25.220
Oddly, that may simply be the result of one
too many people pegging Saulnier's punk-tinged

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.360
chiller as a horror film.

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:31.420
While the story itself is endlessly horrific,
Green Room might've found its audience if

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:34.740
viewers had been expecting a first-rate action
flick instead.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.720
That's not to say there aren't ghouls aplenty
in Green Room.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:38.360
There are.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.060
They just happen to be of the very human Neo-Nazi
persuasion.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:46.340
Their prey, a down-on-their-luck hardcore
band, are equally human.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:51.459
When they inadvertently witness a murder while
on tour, the band's instinct is to hightail

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.400
it out of town.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.740
Their Nazi counterparts have other plans,
which gets even worse when their sneering

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.640
leader shows up and essentially condemns the
band to death.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.899
From that moment on, Green Room breathlessly
propels itself forward with the filth and

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:10.410
the fury of a punk rock thrasher, indulging
in unflinchingly tense showdowns and grueling

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.540
acts of violence every step of the way.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.920
Saulnier delivers an insightful, surprisingly
timely action thriller that'll leave your

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.320
brain racing and your heart pumping overtime.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.720
In case you hadn't noticed, Netflix has been
churning out movies at a feverish rate over

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:25.920
the past several years.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.740
Of course, with their under-the-radar release
strategy and general lack of proper promotion,

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:34.760
it's entirely likely you've missed out on
more of those releases than you thought.

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:40.040
We'd lay even odds that the blisteringly brutal
gangster drama The Night Comes For Us is among

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.060
the films you missed.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.600
If that's the case, it's an oversight that
action fans will want to rectify immediately,

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:49.780
because this blood-soaked crime saga packs
a punch you won't be able to shake.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:55.560
At the center of that saga is a former Triad
enforcer named Ito who, while slaughtering

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.620
an entire village for his bosses, stays the
execution of a young girl.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.660
In doing so, he sets off a bloody chain of
events that puts the Triad on their trail.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:11.160
Matters get insanely violent from that moment
on, with a ceaseless wave of Triad henchman

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:16.551
armed with cool names, cool weapons, and cooler
than cool attitudes taking on Ito and a few

00:10:16.551 --> 00:10:19.010
loyal members of his old crew.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.010
Bones are broken.

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.160
Limbs are severed.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.240
Bodies are set aflame or blown to bits.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:28.320
Through all the grueling, meticulously orchestrated
action, the film also manages to capture a

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.220
deeply human story of redemption.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.720
For action fans of a certain age, the late
'80s remain a fertile time period for a particular

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.380
sort of absurdly predictable and way over
the top action flick.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:45.480
There's no doubt that genre maven Adam Wingard
is one of those fans, and his stylized, horror-tinged

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.840
thriller The Guest is all the proof you'll
ever need.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:52.400
For the uninitiated, being predictable and
over-the-top aren't really hindrances to this

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.180
sort of film.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.160
In fact, the mixture of seriousness and absurdity
is all part of the fun.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.180
Wingard and screenwriter Simon Barrett have
about as much fun with that approach in The

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.340
Guest as any filmmakers have ever had.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:09.580
Leading the charge for the film's giddily
over-the-top vibe is Dan Stevens, who delivers

00:11:09.589 --> 00:11:14.650
a fascinatingly unhinged performance as a
mysterious soldier who befriends an unsuspecting

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:15.140
family.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.620
He's ostensibly there to pay respects to their
son, who supposedly died in combat while serving

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.160
alongside Stevens' character.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:27.820
Without spoiling any of the dizzying madness,
we'll just say that the man isn't who he claims

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:28.320
to be.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:34.640
As bodies start piling up around town, The
Guest twists and turns from eerie stolen identity

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.329
drama into a surreal, neo-noir horror show
rife with all the genre affectations one could

00:11:40.329 --> 00:11:41.300
ever hope for.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:43.200
"You want the elite?"

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.380
"I'll take all of them.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:47.640
“All the handguns?”

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.160
No everything.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:50.960
All your guns."

